 ## Background
-RLSplat was proposed by Bae, Park, and Kim from KAIST in 2026 [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of prior budget-controllable FF3DGS methods like EcoSplat and F4 Splat, which predict dense sets of Gaussians and prune them, resulting in constant inference costs regardless of the target budget [S1, unofficial]. RLSplat introduces a Gaussian allocation policy trained with Group Sequence Policy Optimization (GSPO) and a resource-linear decoder with hierarchical neighbor search to achieve true linear scaling of inference cost with the Gaussian budget [S1, unofficial].
+RLSplat was proposed by Bae, Park, and Kim from KAIST. The work addresses the limitations of prior budget-controllable FF3DGS methods like EcoSplat and F4 Splat, which predict dense sets of Gaussians and then prune them, resulting in constant inference costs [S1, unofficial]. RLSplat introduces a resource-linear approach by formulating Gaussian generation as an allocation problem and using a learned policy to distribute the budget across local scene regions [S1, unofficial]. The method uses Group Sequence Policy Optimization (GSPO) for training the allocation policy [S1, unofficial].
 
